# Phishing Email Analysis — Sample 02

**Source of sample:** rf-peixoto/phishing_pot (GitHub)
**Filename / sample ID:** sample-02.eml

## 1. Email Metadata
| Field | Value |
|---|---|
| Claimed sender | Microsoft account team |
| From address | no-reply@access-accsecurity.com |
| Subject | Microsoft account unusual signin activity |
| Date/time sent | 8 Sep 2023, 05:46:57 UTC |

## 2. Header Analysis
| Check | Result |
|---|---|
| SPF | none |
| DKIM | none |
| DMARC | permerror (malformed DMARC record — a config error, distinct from a transient temperror) |

From domain (access-accsecurity.com) has no relation to Microsoft's real domains (microsoft.com / accountprotection.microsoft.com).

## 3. Link / Infrastructure Analysis
No credential-harvesting login page — all body links use `mailto:sotrecognizd@gmail.com` (a personal Gmail address, not a Microsoft channel), for "Report The User" and "unsubscribe" actions. Contains an invisible 1x1 tracking pixel pointing to thebandalisty.com/track/..., a shared open tracking-pixel service reused across hundreds of unrelated spam/phishing campaigns (731 scans over ~2 years on urlscan.io), now showing a parked/domain-for-sale page.

## 5. Social Engineering Tactics
- [x] Fear/urgency (unusual signin from Russia/Moscow)
- [x] Authority impersonation (Microsoft account team)
- [x] Engagement bait via mailto reply, not credential entry

## 6. Red Flags Summary
1. SPF/DKIM both fully absent (none), DMARC malformed (permerror)
2. Sender domain unrelated to Microsoft
3. All "action" links route to a personal Gmail mailto:, not a Microsoft portal — inconsistent with any real Microsoft workflow
4. Invisible tracking pixel confirms this is address-validation/engagement-harvesting rather than credential phishing

## 7. Verdict
**Classification:** Phishing (reconnaissance/engagement-harvesting variant, not credential-harvesting)
**Confidence:** High
**Analyst summary:** Impersonates Microsoft's account security team to provoke a reply or click confirming the address is live, via mailto-based "actions" rather than a fake login page. Combined with a hidden tracking pixel from a known multi-campaign tracking service, this fits an inbox-validation/targeting pattern rather than direct credential theft.
